  "actual_response": "Create a new workspace-authored fork under `@me`. The supplied resolution is sufficient for this plan: `@example/skills/release-editor` is an acquired Registry package, and its accepted publisher remains `@example`. Its canonical retained directory and `.agents/skills/release-editor` projection are not authoring surfaces for your derivative.\n\n1. In a project authoring workspace, preview a fork of the exact installed package. I suggest a distinct identity, `@me/skills/release-editor-team`, to make the derivative explicit:\n\n   ```sh\n   axm fork ./agent_extensions/registry.example.test/@example/skills/release-editor @me/skills/release-editor-team --preview --json\n   ```\n\n   Review the candidate\u2019s source, new identity, authored destination, and desired-state changes. Preserve the upstream package and accepted publisher binding. `adopt` takes over authorship in place; your separate `@me` identity calls for `fork`. Native import is unnecessary because this is already a managed package. Before a later apply, check CLI compatibility and workspace lint; that future preflight does not replace the supplied resolution in this plan.\n\n2. When execution is authorized, apply the same bounded fork without `--preview`:\n\n   ```sh\n   axm fork ./agent_extensions/registry.example.test/@example/skills/release-editor @me/skills/release-editor-team --json\n   ```\n\n   A new fork starts at version `0.1.0` and disabled unless `--enable` is supplied. Keep it disabled while authoring. Read the actual result and use its reported canonical authored path; do not edit the upstream retained package, generated lockfile, or agent projection.\n\n3. Hand that authored package to the skill-authoring workflow. Define the team\u2019s intended job, triggers, inputs, outputs, authority limits, and failure behavior. Preserve supported behavior outside the requested changes and applicable license/attribution. Update the model-facing `SKILL.md` description and body together; keep the human-facing manifest description and README appropriate to their readers. Fork preserves the existing `SKILL.md` frontmatter, so explicitly choose a distinct agent-facing name such as `release-editor-team` before enabling both skills.\n\n4. Verify the fork\u2019s identity and content, the exact desired entry in `axm.json`, and that upstream accepted resolution in `axm.lock` still binds to `@example`. The authored target does not acquire an external lock binding. Confirm the new target is disabled and has no active projection, while the upstream projection is preserved. Use the local skill inventory and lint, then exercise representative positive requests, paraphrases, an adjacent negative, and missing-input or unsupported-claim behavior. These checks distinguish static validation from observed skill behavior.\n\n5. When the authored result is ready for local activation, preview `axm enable @me/skills/release-editor-team --preview --json`, review the candidate, then apply `axm enable @me/skills/release-editor-team --json`. Verify the reported authored path, desired state, preserved upstream accepted resolution, and each configured agent\u2019s projected outcome. Run `axm list --type skill --json`, `axm lint --json`, and `axm sync --preview --fail-on-change --json` to inspect inventory, structural findings, and convergence. Keep any collision or drift as an unresolved finding instead of overwriting it.\n\nThe team can later distribute the new identity through its chosen repository or Registry workflow. Registry publication is a separate authorized operation. This response is a plan; no fork, authoring, activation, publication, or workspace-state check was executed.",
